- Department: Students in technical or science departments (CSE, SWE, Pharmacy, EEE, Civil, MCT, CIS) likely read academic books more often than those in non-technical or arts departments (English, BBA, JMC, Textile, NFE, Others).  
- Amount of time spent reading books per day: More time spent reading books per day positively correlates with reading academic books most of the time.  
- Reason for reading books: If the reason is "Academic Purpose," more likely to read academic books most of the time. Other reasons like entertainment or vocabulary improvement suggest less academic focus.  
- How to manage books for reading: Borrowing or buying books might indicate more serious academic reading than just using e-books or others, but effect is mild.  
- Number of e-books read last two years: Higher number suggests more reading activity, possibly academic if combined with other features.  
- Number of printed books read last two years: Higher number suggests more reading activity, possibly academic if combined with other features.  
- Favorite books for leisure time: If favorite books are "Non-Fiction" or "Biography," might indicate academic inclination; "Fiction" or "Others" may suggest less academic focus during leisure time.  
- Change felt after reading books: "Increase Academic Performance" strongly correlates with academic reading; other changes less so.  
- Who influenced you to read books: Influence by "Teachers" or "Self-Decision" may correlate with academic reading; "Friends" or "Parents" less so.  
- Which language books do you like to read: Preference for "English" might correlate with academic reading, especially in technical fields.  
- Where do you read printed books: Reading in "Library" or "Home" might indicate academic reading, "During Travel" less so.  
- Do you read Newspaper: May not strongly correlate with academic book reading.  
- Format used for reading books: Preference for "Printed" or "Both" might indicate serious reading habits; "E-books" only might be less indicative.  
- Frequency of reading books: "On everyday basis" or "A few times a week" strongly correlates with reading academic books most of the time; less frequent reading correlates with less academic focus.  

